    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Gao was a centre of manufacturing, iron smelting and trade by about 700 CE, and Arabic writers of the 9th century described it as an important regional power.</li><li>It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> towards the end of the 13th century, regained its independence in the first half of the 15th century, and became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-ali">Sunni Ali</a>, who ruled from 1464 to 1492.</li><li>After the Moroccan invasion of 1591 the invaders made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> their capital, and by 1854 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth">Heinrich Barth</a> found Gao a village of about 300 huts.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Gao and the nearby site of Gao-Saney have more than 100 medieval Arabic inscriptions, the oldest dated to 1042 CE, which record generations of kings and queens in the form of epitaphs.</p></aside>
    </section>

