    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 700</span><span class="rf-text">Gao-Saney is occupied as a centre of iron smelting, manufacturing and trade</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">9th century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">Al-Khwarizmi</a> makes the earliest written mention of Gao, already an important regional power</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">early 10th century</span><span class="rf-text">The Songhay king based at Kukiya moves to the site of Gao Ancien</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">late 13th century</span><span class="rf-text">Gao loses its independence and becomes part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1352</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visits Gao, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>, and stays a month</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1464</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-ali">Sunni Ali</a> Ber begins his rule and makes Gao the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1591</span><span class="rf-text">The Moroccan invasion collapses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> becomes the capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1854</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth">Heinrich Barth</a> finds Gao reduced to a village of about 300 huts</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan.jpg" alt="Tarikh al-Sudan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%AE_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%86.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">إيان, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gao stands on the eastern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a> at the junction with the Tilemsi valley, 320 km east-southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. Archaeological digs at Gao-Saney show that the site was occupied by roughly 700 CE and was a centre of manufacturing, iron smelting and trade with areas as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Pottery from the mounds is similar to that found throughout the region, which indicates that Gao was part of a regional market sphere, and the local character of the ceramic, animal and plant remains suggests that Gao-Saney belonged to a broader indigenous cultural complex of the Niger River. Fragments of North African enamel ware, Andalusian chandelier ware and Islamic glass vessels were also recovered.</p><p>The evidence points to two settlements on the eastern bank: Gao Ancien, within the modern town and east of the Tomb of Askia, and Gao-Saney, about 4 km to the east. Imported pottery and glass suggest that Gao-Saney was occupied between the 8th and 11th centuries. At some point no later than the early 10th century the Songhay king based at Kukiya moved to the site of Gao Ancien, and the kings of this period belonged to a lineage known as Qanda. Arabic geographers called the town Kawkaw or Kuku. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a>, al-Sadi names a legendary founder of the Zuwa dynasty, Zuwa Alayman, who he claims came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> to Kukiya.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leo-africanus.jpg" alt="Leo Africanus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sebastiano_del_Piombo_Portrait_of_a_Humanist.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sebastiano del Piombo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The earliest written mention of Gao is by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">al-Khwarizmi</a>, who wrote in the first half of the 9th century, when Gao was already an important regional power. Around 872 al-Yaqubi described the kingdom of the Kawkaw as the greatest of the realms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, to whose king all the other kingdoms owed allegiance. Ibn al-Faqih, writing about 903, mentioned a caravan route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to ancient Ghana by way of Kawkaw. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a>, writing about 988, that route was abandoned in the reign of Ibn Tulun (868 to 884) after bandits attacked some caravans and blown sand overwhelmed others, and a route through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a> and across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> replaced it. The excavators suggest that local elites made use of long-distance trade rather than being created by it.</p><p>Gao stood highest under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>. By the end of the 16th century it controlled an empire of more than 1,400,000 km2. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a>, who visited between 1506 and 1510, saw a large town without a surrounding wall, full of rich merchants. The Tarikh al-fattash reports that a survey made in the reign of Askiya Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> (1582 to 1586) counted 7,626 houses, not including huts of straw. At an average of 6 people to a house this implies a population of about 45,000, which would make Gao one of the largest cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> at the time.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 10th century Gao was already Muslim by the accounts of Arabic writers. Al-Sadi lists 32 rulers of the Zuwa dynasty and states that in 1009 to 1010 the 15th of them, Zuwa Kusoy, was the first to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. John Hunwick, among others, speculates that the first version of Islam there was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadism">Ibadism</a>. Around 1079 to 1082 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> group, inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, took control of the area and left royal epitaphs in a cemetery at Gao-Saney. Whether these kings appear on the king lists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/za-dynasty">Za dynasty</a> in the chronicles is debated, and Dierk Lange proposes that they represent the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonni-dynasty">Sonni dynasty</a>.</p><p>Towards the end of the 13th century Gao lost its independence and became part of the expanding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>. Sometime in the 14th century Ali Kulun, the first ruler of the Sunni dynasty, rebelled, but the Malians regained control. In the first half of the 15th century Sunni Sulayman Dama threw off Malian rule. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-ali">Sunni Ali</a> Ber (1464 to 1492) greatly enlarged the territory and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>, with Gao as its capital. Askiya Muhammad I, the first ruler of the Askiya dynasty, ruled the town when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> visited. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroccan-invasion-of-songhai">Moroccan invasion of 1591</a> the invaders made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> their capital.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Al-Muhallabi, who died in 990, described Gao as two towns. The king lived in the town west of the river with his men and those he trusted, where a mosque stood and where he prayed, while the town on the eastern bank, which he called Sarnah, had markets and trading houses. The communal prayer ground lay between them. Al-Muhallabi wrote that the king and most of his people only pretended to be Muslims. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who spent a month in Gao in 1352 to 1353, called it one of the finest, biggest and most fertile cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, with plenty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, milk, chickens, fish and cucumbers, and recorded that its people bought and sold with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowries</a>, as the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> did. He left with a caravan that included 600 slave girls.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a> found the town very civilised compared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, with bread, meat, melons, cucumbers, squash and rice in abundance, many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> of sweet water, and no wine or fruit. On market days large numbers of male and female slaves were sold in a square, and he gave the price of a girl of fifteen as about six ducats. Today the people of Gao mostly speak Koyraboro Senni, and they include Bozo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> keepers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and Bambara from western Mali. The city is divided into nine quartiers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth.jpg" alt="Heinrich Barth" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth">Heinrich Barth</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barth_Unterschrift.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> recorded that in 1374 to 1375 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> fought a war over Gao against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takedda">Takedda</a>, and the war devastated the city. It did not regain its former importance until the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroccan-invasion-of-songhai">Moroccan invasion of 1591</a> collapsed that empire, and the invaders chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> as their capital, after which the importance of Gao declined. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-barth">Heinrich Barth</a> visited in 1854 he found about 300 huts of matting in clusters. The inhabitants were very poor and owned only a few boats because they had no wood to build them, and the site of the ancient town was overgrown with Capparis decidua bushes.</p><p>On 31 March 2012, one day after the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kidal">Kidal</a>, Malian military forces retreated from Gao's bases and the MNLA and Ansar Dine occupied the city. On 6 April the MNLA declared the region independent of Mali as Azawad. On 26 and 27 June 2012 fighting between the MNLA and the Movement for Oneness and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">Jihad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> ended with the MNLA driven from the city. French warplanes bombed parts of Gao in January 2013, and French and Malian forces captured the city on 26 January 2013. On 18 January 2017 a suicide bomber drove a vehicle filled with explosives into a military camp near Gao and killed 77 people.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gao left a body of writing that questions the claim that no indigenous written records survive from before the middle of the 17th century. Over 100 medieval Arabic inscriptions come from Gao and Gao-Saney, the oldest dated to 1042 CE, with poetry and the epitaphs of many generations of kings and queens. A letter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askia-muhammad">Askia Muhammad I</a>, quoted in al-Maghili's reply, adds details of 15th-century social and economic life. The two 17th-century chronicles, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a> and the Tarikh al-fattash, cover the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> period, though their accounts before the second half of the 15th century are likely to be less reliable.</p><p>The mosque-tomb of Askia Muhammad, built between 1493 and 1538 and listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as the Tomb of Askia, covered about 2500 m2 and had seven bays parallel to the qibla wall and a courtyard with a minaret 12 metres high. Excavations in Old Gao also uncovered a mosque of the early 10th century. Gao is today a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, the capital of the Gao Region and the largest town in the east of the country, and it is regarded as a Songhay cultural capital. The urban commune had 86,633 people at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2009.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Gao, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, was occupied by about 700 CE and became a trading centre, then the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-ali">Sunni Ali</a>. It declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroccan-invasion-of-songhai">Moroccan invasion of 1591</a> and is now the capital of the Gao Region.</em></p>

